// TOOLCHAIN REPRO (not a yyjson feature) — an effect-branch event's own
// declared parameter name gets reused VERBATIM as the inlined body's local
// binding name at the call site, with no hygiene/renaming. If the caller
// happens to already have a local bound to that same identifier (for any
// unrelated reason, anywhere earlier in the same flow function), Zig's
// no-shadowing rule turns it into a hard compile error — even though the
// caller's code and the library's code are both individually correct.
//
// This is a pure core-language finding, nothing yyjson-specific: reproduced
// here with two trivial events that have nothing to do with JSON. See
// yyjson/README.md "Toolchain findings" for the full writeup and the real
// diagnostics from the actual yyjson/index.kz collision that surfaced this
// (array.each's `v: *Value` parameter vs. a caller binding also named `v`
// from an earlier `root(doc): v`).
//
// `get-count` returns i32 directly (`-> i32`), destructured into a binding
// named `v` — completely unremarkable, ordinary caller code. `each-i32` is
// an EFFECT-BRANCH event (`! item`) whose own parameter is ALSO named `v`,
// coincidentally. Calling it downstream of the `v` binding should be totally
// fine (they're unrelated variables in unrelated code) — instead the build
// fails because the inliner's substitution for `each-i32`'s own `v` parameter
// collides with the caller's `v`.
//
// EXPECTED (if the hygiene gap is fixed): compiles and runs, printing
//   count: 3
//   saw 10
//   saw 20
//   saw 30
//
// ACTUAL (current compiler): fails to build with:
//   output_emitted.zig:N:M: error: local constant 'v' shadows local constant from outer scope
//   note: previous declaration here
//
// Reproduce:
//   koruc run yyjson/tests/TOOLCHAIN_REPRO_effect_param_shadow.kz

~import std/io

~pub tor get-count { n: i32 } -> i32
~proc get-count|zig {
    return n;
}

~pub tor each-i32 { v: []const i32 }
! item i32

~proc each-i32|zig {
    for (v) |it| item(it);
}

~get-count(n: 3): v |> std/io:print.ln("count: {{ v:d }}") |> each-i32(v: &[_]i32{ 10, 20, 30 })
! item x |> std/io:print.ln("saw {{ x:d }}")
